      "message": "**1. Weakened Bitmask Assertion in Helper Function**\n* **Location:** `trigger_match_attempt(uint32_t mode)`\n* **Issue:** The new assertion `zassert_true(mode_resp.mode \u0026 mode);` introduces a regression in test strictness. In tests where multiple flags are passed (e.g., `test_match_no_template_update`, which passes `FP_MODE_MATCH | FP_MODE_MATCH_NO_TEMPLATE_UPDATE`), the bitwise `\u0026` operator will evaluate to `true` if *any* of the requested bits are present in `mode_resp.mode`. \n    * *Original code behavior:* Explicitly asserted both bits were set independently. \n    * *New code behavior:* If the sensor fails to set `FP_MODE_MATCH_NO_TEMPLATE_UPDATE` but successfully sets `FP_MODE_MATCH`, the assertion will still pass, masking a potential failure.\n* **Remediation:** Ensure that *all* requested bits are set by modifying the assertion to compare the masked result against the original input:\n    ```c\n    zassert_equal(mode_resp.mode \u0026 mode, mode, \"Failed to set all requested FP modes\");\n    ```\n\n---\n\n### **Minor Issues and Suggestions**\n\n**1. Uninitialized Struct on Stack**\n* **Location:** `test_match_no_templates_mode_cleared()`\n* **Issue:** The refactor changed `struct ec_params_fp_mode params \u003d { .mode \u003d FP_MODE_MATCH, };` to an uninitialized declaration: `struct ec_params_fp_mode params;`. While you later set `params.mode \u003d FP_MODE_DONT_CHANGE;`, leaving the rest of the struct uninitialized on the stack can lead to passing garbage data or uninitialized padding bytes to `ec_cmd_fp_mode()`. This can occasionally cause flaky behavior depending on the underlying ABI or future modifications to the struct.\n* **Remediation:** Initialize the struct when declaring it, or zero it out completely:\n    ```c\n    struct ec_params_fp_mode params \u003d {0};\n    // Then later:\n    params.mode \u003d FP_MODE_DONT_CHANGE;\n    ```\n\n---",
